# Final test: how far have you come?
teaches: ["safety/hanging-pieces","tactics/fork","tactics/pin","tactics/mating-patterns"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How do you score now, against the same kind of test as on day 1? What should the next three months of training look like?"

## Warm-up: a skewer and a smothered mate
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 8/5p2/2k4r/6pp/8/6P1/5PKP/R7 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ra6+, Kd5, Rxh6]}
      - {fen: r4r1k/pp1b2pp/1q5N/8/2Q5/2P5/PP3PPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qg8+, Rxg8, Nf7#]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: How far have you come since day one? Today you find out, with a test of the same kind.
    draw position

B: Warm me up first.
    point at.ref piece_a1 gesture circle

A: Two puzzles from the weeks in between. A rook ending first.
    point at.ref piece_c6 gesture circle

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A check can hit two things on one line.","Your rook."] themes ["skewer"] voice a teaches ["tactics/skewer"]
        right: Rook to a6, check. The king steps off the sixth rank to d5, and rook takes h6.
        wrong: Find a check along a line with the king in front and something behind it.

B: A check, and the rook behind the king falls. A skewer.
    point at.ref sq_h6 gesture circle

    pause 0.3

A: Second one. Black's king is boxed in by his own men.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

B: Queen to g8 is check, but the rook just takes her.
    point at.ref sq_g8 gesture circle

A: Then look at what's left around his king.
    highlight piece_h8 piece_g7 piece_h7

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true hints ["A queen sacrifice with check.","Then your knight."] themes ["smothered mate","mating pattern"] voice a
        right: Queen to g8, check. The rook has to take her, and knight to f7 is mate: the king is smothered by his own men.
        wrong: After the rook takes on g8, which squares are left for the king?

B: Thirty days ago I'd never have given up the queen.
    highlight piece_g8

A: Patterns like these are new. The test shows whether the habit is new too.
    fade position to 0.4

    erase position

A: Today: how to take the test,
    text map_how "how to take it" x 500 y 470 font_size 60

A: the test itself,
    text map_test "the test" x 500 y 390 font_size 60

A: and the next three months.
    text map_months "3 months" x 500 y 310 font_size 60

## Before you move, and after
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bqkbnr/ppp2ppp/2n5/3pp3/8/4PN2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [Bd3, e4]}
      - {fen: 4r1k1/pp3ppp/8/3n4/8/1B6/PP3PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxd5, Re1#], highlights: [d5]}
      - {fen: 4r1k1/pp3ppp/8/3n4/8/1B6/PP3PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxd5], highlights: []}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    text t_notseen "not seen" x 320 y 330 font_size 60 hidden true
    text t_toofast "too fast" x 680 y 330 font_size 60 hidden true
    pieces six x 500 y 440 width 600 height 110 n 6 of strips hidden true
    text t_lucky "lucky?" x 545 y 550 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Before you move, and after"

A: Now that you're warm, back to the very first position of day one.
    draw position

B: Bishop to d3. I played it without a look.
    point at.ref piece_f1 gesture circle

A: Play it again, and this time ask Black's checks, captures and threats. Which black move hurts?
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.6

    quiz "After bishop to d3, which Black move hurts?" on_canvas true buttons_layout column buttons_at.x 915 buttons_at.y 375 id dq keep_buttons true voice a
        right: Pawn to e4 attacks the bishop and the knight at once, so one of them is lost.
        wrong: Look for one black move that attacks two of your pieces.
        + pawn e4
        * knight f6
        * bishop g4

    mark dq_x2 around dq_btn_2 kind strike
    mark dq_x3 around dq_btn_3 kind strike

B: Two pieces hit, and only one can run.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.4

    highlight piece_d3 piece_f3

A: That's the first habit: before you move, look at his answers, not just your idea.
    point at.ref piece_e4 gesture circle

    erase dq_btn_1 dq_btn_2 dq_btn_3 dq_x2 dq_x3

    pause 0.4

A: Now a new one. Black's knight on d5 has no defender.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

B: Rook takes it. That rook's been doing nothing on d1.
    point at.ref piece_d1 gesture circle

A: It was doing one thing. Take, and then ask his checks.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.6

A: Rook to e1 is mate. Your rook was the only guard of the back rank.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.6

    pause 0.5

A: Same knight. Win it, and keep the game.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Which of your pieces can leave its post safely?","Your bishop."] themes ["hanging piece","back rank"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces","safety/back-rank"]
        right: Bishop takes d5. Your rook stays on d1 and still guards e1.
        wrong: Take the knight with the piece that guards nothing.

A: That's the second habit: once you've chosen, ask his checks, captures and threats once more.
    highlight piece_d1

A: It's called a blunder check.
    point at.ref sq_e1 gesture circle

B: So if I beat my day-one score, I've improved?
    erase position

A: Probably. But six positions is a small sample:
    draw six

A: one lucky guess moves the score.
    draw t_lucky

    erase six t_lucky

A: So mark each miss as well: not seen, or seen but played too fast.
    draw t_notseen t_toofast

B: Not seen means I need more patterns. Too fast means the habit slipped.
    highlight t_notseen t_toofast

A: Yes. Your misses tell you what to train next.
    highlight t_notseen t_toofast effect underline

## The final test
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/2np4/8/3Pn3/2N2N2/PPP1BPPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxe4]}
      - {fen: r1b1kb1r/pp1q1ppp/3p1nn1/4p3/2B1P3/2N2N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 0 1, moves: [Bb5, Qxb5, Nxb5]}
      - {fen: r4rk1/pbp1qppp/1p1p4/8/4Pn2/2Q2N2/PPP2PPP/R1B2RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Ne2+, Kh1, Nxc3, bxc3], flip: true}
      - {fen: 3r2k1/pp3ppp/q7/8/8/8/PP1Q1PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxd8#]}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/pp2bppp/2nppn2/6N1/3PN3/3Q4/PPPB1PPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxf6+, Bxf6, Qxh7#]}
      - {fen: 8/8/8/8/P7/5k2/8/7K b - - 0 1, moves: [Ke4], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bqkbnr/ppp2ppp/2n5/3pp3/8/4PN2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [Bd3, e4]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: capablanca
      title: Last Lectures
      authors: José Raúl Capablanca
      year: 1966
      finding: 'To improve, study the endgame before everything else: the endings can be studied and mastered by themselves, the middlegame and opening only in relation to the endgame.'
      url: https://en.wikiquote.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Ra%C3%BAl_Capablanca

    text test_label "Day 30" x 900 y 560 font_size 36 hidden true
    text score_big "Day 30 score" x 500 y 540 font_size 80 hidden true
    text day1_cue "Day 1:  __ / 6" x 500 y 400 font_size 60 hidden true
    polygon pawn_square #b5482e points [[422.5,65],[810,65],[810,452.5],[422.5,452.5]] style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    text plan_seen "not seen → daily puzzles" x 500 y 500 font_size 50 hidden true
    text plan_fast "too fast → slow games" x 500 y 400 font_size 50 hidden true
    text plan_review "+ review each game" x 500 y 330 font_size 44 hidden true
    text plan_endings "endings" cites ["capablanca"] x 905 y 560 font_size 40 hidden true
    text syn_hope "hope" x 905 y 580 font_size 44 hidden true
    mark hope_strike around syn_hope kind strike hidden true
    text q_checks "checks" x 905 y 470 font_size 36 hidden true
    text q_captures "captures" x 905 y 410 font_size 36 hidden true
    text q_threats "threats" x 905 y 350 font_size 36 hidden true
    text hw_game "one slow game: 15 + 10" x 500 y 470 font_size 52 hidden true
    text hw_mistake "find its first mistake" x 500 y 390 font_size 52 hidden true
    text bye "see you over the board" x 500 y 260 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "The final test"

A: Now that you know what to watch for: the test.
    draw position

A: Six positions, the same kinds as day one, and a few from the weeks since.
    draw test_label

A: One move each, or a short line. Three questions first, then the blunder check.
    highlight test_label effect underline

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true themes ["hanging piece"] hints ["Captures first: what is loose?","Your knight on c3."] voice a test day30
        right: Knight takes e4. Black's knight stood there with no defender.

A: Two.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true themes ["pin"] hints ["Line up the queen and the king.","Your bishop."] voice a test day30
        right: Bishop to b5 pins the queen to her king, and she's lost: she takes the bishop, and your knight takes her.

A: Three. You're Black, so the board turns.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true themes ["fork"] hints ["Checks first.","Your knight on f4."] voice a test day30
        right: Knight to e2, check, hits the king and the queen. The king steps to h1, the knight takes the queen, and the pawn takes back: a queen for a knight.

A: Four: White again.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true themes ["back rank"] hints ["Look at Black's back rank.","Your queen."] voice a test day30 teaches ["safety/back-rank"]
        right: Queen takes d8, mate. Nothing can take her, and the king's own pawns hold him in.

A: Five.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true themes ["removing the defender","mating pattern"] hints ["Who guards h7?","Take the guard, with check."] voice a test day30 teaches ["tactics/removing-the-defender","tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Knight takes f6, check, and the guard of h7 is gone. Whatever Black does, queen takes h7 is mate.

A: Last one: a pawn ending, and you're Black.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    ask "Stop the pawn." widget chess line true themes ["square rule"] hints ["Draw the pawn's square.","Your king."] voice a test day30 teaches ["endgames/square-rule"]
        right: King to e4. Your king catches the pawn before it queens.

A: The pawn's square runs from a4 to e8. Step inside it, and you're in time.
    draw pawn_square

    erase test_label pawn_square

    erase position

    draw score_big

A: You scored {day30.score} of {day30.total}.
    results test day30

A: Now look at what you wrote down on day one.
    draw day1_cue

B: And the ones I missed today?
    point at.ref day1_cue gesture underline

A: They come back now, with any you missed on earlier days.
    explore activity review_misses

    erase score_big day1_cue

A: Then the next three months.
    text plan_title "next 3 months" x 500 y 600 font_size 60

A: Misses you never saw: ten or fifteen minutes of puzzles every day, so the patterns stay.
    draw plan_seen

A: Misses you played too fast: slow games every week,
    draw plan_fast

A: each one reviewed. Find the first mistake yourself, before the engine.
    draw plan_review

    erase plan_seen plan_fast plan_review plan_title

    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 1 duration 0.1

A: And endings, like the pawn race in the test.
    draw position pawn_square

A: Capablanca, a world champion, advised studying the endings before anything else.
    draw plan_endings

    erase pawn_square plan_endings

A: Back to day one's first position.
    animate position_shown 6 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

A: On day one you played your move and hoped.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.4

    draw syn_hope

    draw hope_strike

A: Now you ask three questions, for both sides, before and after.
    draw q_checks q_captures q_threats

A: Asked here, they find this reply before you ever play bishop to d3.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.4

    erase position syn_hope hope_strike q_checks q_captures q_threats

A: Homework, one last time: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower,
    draw hw_game

A: and find its first mistake.
    draw hw_mistake

A: That's thirty days. Well done, and see you over the board.
    draw bye

## Where next
end_page: true

    link end_again href lecture:30 title "Watch again" form text mark replay open here x 500 y 400 font_size 28
    text end_count "30 of 30" x 500 y 330 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
